-- ============================================================================
-- PD9 PROFESSIONAL DEEP SEED: Production Surveillance, intermediate tier.
--
-- Six modules, 26 lessons, 132 questions (15 per module bank plus a 42
-- question final exam). The structure row is GENERATED from the tier's
-- manifest.json rather than hand typed.
--
-- What this tier owns: THE WINDOWED READING AND WHAT IT FIRES, plus THE WELL
-- TEST THAT CARRIES A WELL and THE ALLOCATION THAT SHARES ONE METER OVER MANY
-- WELLS. Three of its six graded fields are `windowMean` results read out of
-- `detectExceptions`, one is an allocation factor, one is an allocated volume
-- and one is an imbalance percentage. Every one of the six is a COMPARISON: a
-- recent window against a baseline window on the same well, or a metered total
-- against what the wells were capable of, or a meter against what the wells
-- actually booked. The tier is the whole of the domain that puts two numbers
-- side by side and reports the difference.
--
-- THE SPLIT IS IN THE CODE. The Associate tier is every function that reads
-- rows; this tier is every function that holds TWO windows, or holds a METER
-- and a set of TESTS. `detectExceptions` is the first function in
-- `surveillance.js` that compares anything, and `computeAllocation` is the
-- whole of `allocation.js` that divides one number by another. Neither
-- function introduces a new physical quantity: both are arithmetic on the same
-- columns the tier below already named. What is new is that an answer now
-- depends on a WINDOW and on a CHOICE OF BASIS, and neither the window nor the
-- basis is visible in the number that comes back.
--
-- WHERE A WINDOW STARTS, EXACTLY, BECAUSE EVERY NUMBER IN THIS TIER SITS ON
-- ONE. All windows anchor on the FIELD's latest ledger date and never on the
-- wall clock, which is the module's own decision and a good one: a three year
-- old dataset surveils honestly instead of declaring every well stale.
-- `windowMean` then takes days strictly GREATER than its from-day and less
-- than or equal to its to-day, so the recent window is the last `recentDays`
-- rows and the baseline is the `baselineDays` rows immediately before them,
-- with no overlap and no gap. `computeKpis` cuts differently, at
-- asOf - windowDays + 1 and INCLUSIVE, so a seven day KPI window and a seven
-- day exception window hold the same seven rows by two different inequalities.
-- Getting that right is not pedantry: it is the difference between a mean over
-- seven rows and a mean over eight.
--
-- AND THE WINDOWS WIDEN FOR A COARSE LEDGER, WHICH IS THE RIGHT FIX FOR THE
-- WRONG HALF OF THE PROBLEM. `seriesCadenceDays` is the MEDIAN gap between
-- consecutive rows, and the recent, baseline and stale windows are each
-- stretched to cover enough points on a monthly ledger. Silently comparing one
-- month against one month would be the worst failure mode available, because
-- it looks like it worked. But NOTHING IN A LEDGER ROW SAYS HOW LONG THE ROW
-- COVERS, and while the windows widen the VOLUMES are never rescaled. On the
-- teaching monthly well a period volume falls from 23200 stb over thirty days
-- to 11600 stb over fourteen, which is 773.333333333 stb/d rising to
-- 828.571428571 stb/d, and the engine reports "Oil down 52%" at HIGH severity,
-- the top of the ladder, for producing MORE oil per day than it did before.
--
-- WHAT ACTUALLY FIRES, AND THE LADDER IS NOT WHAT IT LOOKS LIKE. There are
-- seven exception types and FIVE of them can reach high: `shut_in`
-- unconditionally, and `rate_drop`, `injection_drop`, `watercut_rise` and
-- `gor_rise` by doubling their own trigger. Only `downtime` and `stale_data`
-- cannot. Count the TYPES that can reach high, not the high rows a particular
-- field happens to show, because those are two different numbers. `shut_in` is
-- always high whatever the size of the well, `downtime` is always medium
-- whatever the hours, and doubling on `stale_data` takes it only from info to
-- medium, so a well silent for four hundred days ranks BELOW a forty per cent
-- rate drop and is ordered among the mediums alphabetically. `stale_data` also
-- RETURNS EARLY, so no other comparison on that well is ever made: a well that
-- went quiet is not also reported as having collapsed, because the function
-- stopped looking.
--
-- THE THREE DIALS THE TIER HAS TO BE ABLE TO NAME. `rateDropPct` is 20 with a
-- doubling to high at 40. `watercutRisePts` is 10 POINTS and not a percentage,
-- with high at 20 points. `gorRisePct` is 30 PER CENT and not points, with
-- high at 60. `downtimeHours` is 12 and produces a medium and only a medium.
-- `staleDays` is 7. `minOilRate` is 5 stb/d. A learner who cannot say which of
-- those decided a given row has not read the row.
--
-- THREE THINGS THE TIER MUST NOT LET A LEARNER ASSUME.
--
--   A WELL TEST CARRIES A WELL BECAUSE OF A FLAG, NOT BECAUSE IT IS ANY GOOD.
--   `groupTests` drops a test only when `is_valid` is STRICTLY false, so an
--   absent flag, a null, a zero and the string "false" are all KEPT: five
--   spellings of six survive. `testInForce` then takes the most recent test on
--   or before the day, within `maxTestAgeDays`. Meanwhile `validateWellTests`
--   runs a real QC in the SAME FILE and its verdict is fed nowhere at all: on
--   the published field the test in force is one the same file's QC calls HIGH
--   severity on three separate codes, and it carries the well regardless. The
--   QC also returns only the tests WITH issues and no count of tests checked,
--   so an empty array means either "every test is clean" or "nothing ran", and
--   the caller cannot tell which. And the outlier check cannot see a well's
--   first three tests at all, because it needs three PRIOR ones and takes its
--   median over the earlier tests only, so a well's first bad test is never
--   caught and instead becomes part of the median that judges the later ones.
--
--   AN ALLOCATION FACTOR IS THE OUTPUT AND NOT AN INTERNAL, AND CLOSURE IS
--   NARROWER THAN IT SOUNDS. The theoretical is the test rate times the
--   uptime, the factor is the metered total over the sum of theoreticals, and
--   every well's share is its theoretical times that one factor. Nothing
--   normalises a factor to one and nothing clamps it into the warning band:
--   the band raises a diagnostic and the number is reported as it fell out,
--   which is the decision that makes an allocation defensible. Because every
--   share is the same factor times a theoretical, an allocated DAY closes
--   exactly. But a date whose theoretical is ZERO has no factor, is not
--   allocated at all, and its metered volume is simply NOT in the grand
--   allocated: the published no-basis case loses 310 stb that way. There is no
--   closure figure anywhere in the return, so a consumer who wants to know
--   whether the field closed has to subtract two grand totals itself, and
--   nothing prompts it to.
--
--   AND AN IMBALANCE IS A THIRD NUMBER, NOT EITHER OF THE FIRST TWO. The
--   factor compares the meter against what the wells were CAPABLE of. The
--   imbalance compares the meter against what the wells BOOKED. They answer
--   different questions and they move for different reasons, and on a field
--   where a well stops filing rows while the meter keeps seeing it, one of
--   them moves and the other does not. `imbalanceSeries` divides by the BOOKED
--   volume, so the percentage is undefined on a day the wells booked nothing
--   and it is not symmetric in the two quantities.
--
-- TWO SMALLER ONES THE TIER OWNS BECAUSE THIS IS WHERE THEY ARE MET.
--   ANY UNRECOGNISED WELL TYPE IS READ AS A PRODUCER. `detectExceptions`
--   filters out `observation` and branches on the exact string `injector`;
--   `water_injector`, `gas_injector`, an upper case `INJECTOR`, an empty
--   string and an absent type all take the PRODUCER path, get compared on
--   their oil column and get measured against `minOilRate`. `well_type` is
--   whatever the importing system called it.
--   AND THE NODAL CROSS-CHECK FALLS BACK TO THE MODEL FOR ONE RATE CONDITION
--   AND TO ZERO FOR THE NEXT. Two adjacent lines: the water cut falls back to
--   the well model's own, and the gas-oil ratio falls back to a DEAD-OIL zero
--   rather than to the model's. So a test that recorded a rate and no gas is
--   solved against a well the function has just decided makes no gas. The
--   deviation also divides by the NODAL rate, so the same absolute gap reads
--   differently depending on which way round the two are: on the published
--   cases 711.0775931636508 stb/d below the model reads as -54.23611820318044
--   per cent and 588.9224068363492 stb/d above it reads as +44.91895902326194.
--
-- WHAT THIS TIER MUST NOT PROMISE, AND IT IS HANDED STRAIGHT UPWARD. EVERY
-- NUMBER ABOVE IS A READING OF ONE COLUMN OVER ONE WINDOW BY ONE OF SEVERAL
-- FUNCTIONS THAT FORM IT DIFFERENTLY, AND NOTHING IN ANY RETURN SAYS WHICH.
-- That can be stated honestly here without a word of Expert material, because
-- it is visible in the shape of the returns themselves: an exception carries a
-- value and a baseline and no window, an allocation day carries a factor and
-- no basis, and a KPI object carries a watercut with no note that another
-- function in the same file forms the same watercut another way. What that is
-- worth, in barrels and in printed severities, is the tier above.
--
-- The only numbers that travel to the tier above are the field's own stated
-- conditions. The Expert tier restates all of them and re-runs every call for
-- itself.
-- ============================================================================

insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
values ('surveillance', 'intermediate', '{"modules":[{"key":"m01-two-windows-on-one-well","title":"Two Windows on One Well","lesson_keys":["l01-why-a-well-is-compared-to-itself","l02-where-the-windows-start-and-end","l03-anchored-on-the-data","l04-widening-for-a-coarse-ledger","l05-what-a-window-assumes"]},{"key":"m02-the-seven-exceptions","title":"The Seven Exceptions","lesson_keys":["l01-a-drop-and-a-stop","l02-the-two-ratio-triggers","l03-downtime-and-stale-data","l04-how-a-severity-is-assigned","l05-reading-a-surveillance-list"]},{"key":"m03-the-test-that-carries-a-well","title":"The Test That Carries a Well","lesson_keys":["l01-which-test-is-in-force","l02-ageing-a-test-out","l03-what-the-qc-checks","l04-what-the-qc-decides"]},{"key":"m04-one-meter-many-wells","title":"One Meter, Many Wells","lesson_keys":["l01-why-an-allocation-is-not-a-measurement","l02-theoretical-rate-and-uptime","l03-the-factor-is-the-output","l04-closure-and-what-it-proves","l05-monthly-factors-and-the-write-back"]},{"key":"m05-what-the-meter-and-the-model-say","title":"What the Meter and the Model Say","lesson_keys":["l01-the-imbalance-an-engineer-chases","l02-checking-a-test-against-its-well","l03-deferments-and-the-loss-book","l04-what-a-diagnostic-is-for"]},{"key":"m06-the-professional-reading","title":"The Professional Reading","lesson_keys":["l01-the-story-so-far","l02-working-the-capstone","l03-onward"]}]}'::jsonb, 1)
on conflict (app_slug, tier) do update
  set structure = excluded.structure,
      content_version = excluded.content_version,
      active = true,
      updated_at = now();

delete from public.academy_quiz_questions
 where app_slug = 'surveillance' and tier = 'intermediate';

insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-two-windows-on-one-well', 1, 'At the defaults and an asOf of 2025-06-30, the recent window runs 2025-06-24 to 2025-06-30 and the baseline runs 2025-05-25 to 2025-06-23. Which window holds the row dated 2025-06-23?', '["Both, since the boundary day is shared and its value is averaged into each window before the two means are compared.", "The recent window, because a recent window of 7 days has to reach back 7 days from the anchor and so starts the day after the baseline ends.", "The baseline window, because both windows are half open at the early end so the boundary day falls to the earlier of the two.", "Neither, because a day on a window boundary is skipped so that the two means are built from strictly interior rows."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The recent window is (asOf - recentDays, asOf] and the baseline is (asOf - recentDays - baselineDays, asOf - recentDays], so 2025-06-23 belongs to exactly one of them and it is the baseline.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-two-windows-on-one-well', 2, 'Widening recentDays from 7 to 21 on the published field, everything else left alone, takes the list from 9 exceptions to 4. What happens to the high rows?', '["There are none left: all 4 of the remaining exceptions come back at medium, because a mean over a longer window is a weaker detector of a change that happened inside it.", "The 4 high rows survive and the 5 medium rows are the ones a longer mean absorbs first.", "Two remain high, since a longer mean is steadier and crosses a doubled trigger more confidently.", "The high count is unchanged, because widening a window changes which rows are averaged and not which thresholds they meet."]'::jsonb, 0, 'At a recentDays of 14 the list is already down to 6 exceptions with a single high, and the bad days have not gone anywhere: they are averaged against more ordinary ones.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-two-windows-on-one-well', 3, 'Sweeping baselineDays across 7, 14, 30, 60, 90 and 180 on the published field returns 9 exceptions with 4 high and 5 medium at every one of the six. What does that show?', '["That baselineDays is capped internally at 30 days, so any setting above the default is silently clamped back before the mean is taken.", "That baselineDays is never read, because the comparison is against a fixed standard rate rather than a second window.", "That the two windows are the same length, so their ratio cannot move.", "The baseline length is inert on this field, while the recent length takes the same list from 9 exceptions to 3."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The baseline mean genuinely moves at each of the six settings, and every comparison stays on the same side of its trigger, so the printed list is identical at 9.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-two-windows-on-one-well', 4, 'Where does the asOf that anchors every window come from?', '["Today''s date, which is why an old dataset comes back with every well reported stale.", "The latest ledger date on the field handed in, so the same rows surveil the same way whenever the run is made.", "The latest ledger date on each well, so each well is compared against its own most recent row.", "The asOf argument alone, and the function refuses to run when it is omitted."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The teaching field OGUTA, which this course invented and which is neither published nor real, runs to 2024-11-20 and anchors there, so its windows are 2024-11-14 to 2024-11-20 and 2024-10-15 to 2024-11-13.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-two-windows-on-one-well', 5, 'OGUTA-17 files its last ledger row on 2024-10-28 while the field runs to 2024-11-20, and the engine reports stale_data at medium with a value of 23.000000000000 against a baseline of 7.000000000000. What is that 23 measured against?', '["The field''s latest ledger date, not the well''s own history.", "The well''s own median reporting gap, so a well that normally files monthly would not be reported at 23 days.", "The date the run was made, which is why the number grows on its own between one run and the next.", "The end of the baseline window, since staleness is the distance from the earlier of the two windows."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The staleness check is the one comparison in the function that measures a well against the FIELD anchor, and the message says so: no data for 23 days, field ledger runs to 2024-11-20.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-two-windows-on-one-well', 6, 'Raising staleDays to 30 on the teaching field takes the list from 8 exceptions to 7 and OGUTA-17 raises nothing of any kind. Why is that worse than it looks?', '["The well is reported at info instead, a rank most readers skip, so the row is present and quietly demoted rather than gone.", "Its rows leave the field means, so every remaining severity shifts a little.", "Absence from a list reads exactly like a well that is fine, and no row says a check was turned down.", "The setting also widens both comparison windows on that well."]'::jsonb, 2, 'At a staleDays of 21 the same well is still listed at info; at 30 and at 60 it raises no exception at all, and the list holds 7 rows either way.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-two-windows-on-one-well', 7, 'A well whose median gap between consecutive ledger points is 30.000000 days is handed to detectExceptions at the default settings. What windows does it get?', '["recentDays of 7, baselineDays of 30 and staleDays of 7, since the widening applies to the field cadence rather than to a single well''s.", "recentDays of 45, baselineDays of 120 and staleDays of 45.", "recentDays of 30, baselineDays of 30 and staleDays of 30, the cadence substituted for each of the three defaults.", "recentDays of 45, baselineDays of 120 and staleDays of 7, because the staleness check keeps its default so a silent well is still caught quickly."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Each length is a maximum: recentDays becomes ceil(cadence x 1.5), baselineDays ceil(cadence x 4) and staleDays ceil(cadence x 1.5), and at a cadence of 7.0 days the same rule gives 11, 30 and 11.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-two-windows-on-one-well', 8, 'seriesCadenceDays on a well whose gaps are 1, 1, 30 and 30 days returns 15.500000. What is wrong with reading that as how often the well reports?', '["It is the arithmetic mean of all four gaps, which a single long outage can drag upward without any change in reporting habit.", "It is the largest gap seen, so it describes the worst week of the series and overstates every other one.", "It is measured in days while the widening rule expects hours, so the number is right and the unit it is used in is not.", "It is the mean of the two middle gaps of an even count, so it is a cadence no gap in that series ever was."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The function takes the MEDIAN gap, and a median over an even count averages the two middle values, so 15.500000 sits between a daily habit and a monthly one and matches neither.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-two-windows-on-one-well', 9, 'OGUTA-14 files a period row of 23200.000000 stb covering 30 days and then one of 11600.000000 stb covering 14 days. The engine reports rate_drop at HIGH, value = 11600.000000000 against a baseline of 24400.000000000. What did the well actually do?', '["It produced more oil per elapsed day, 828.571428571 stb/d against 773.333333333 stb/d.", "It halved its output, which is what the 52 per cent in the message measures once the shorter period is taken into account.", "It stopped for part of the last period, which is the only reading under which a fall of that size and a shorter row are the same event.", "It held its output flat, so the change is an artefact of the baseline window covering more months than the recent one."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The module reads every ledger row as a calendar day and never asks how long a row covers, so the PERIOD volume fell while the oil per elapsed day rose, and the message prints stb/d after a number that is a month of stb.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-two-windows-on-one-well', 10, 'The widening rule stretches recentDays, baselineDays and staleDays for a coarse cadence. What does it not do?', '["Stretch the baseline window, which keeps its 30 day default so the comparison always has a fixed reference length behind it.", "Apply to injectors, whose winj column is read on the field cadence rather than on the well''s own.", "Change staleDays, which counts rows.", "Rescale the volumes, because nothing in a ledger row says what period the row covers."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The published monthly well returns an oilPd of 15000.000000 on a row of 15000.000000 stb over a PERIOD, so the widening corrects the windows for a coarse cadence and can never correct the volumes.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-two-windows-on-one-well', 11, 'A well whose entire ledger predates the baseline window is handed to detectExceptions. What comes back for it?', '["A rate_drop of 100 per cent, since a recent mean of nothing against a real baseline is the largest fall the comparison can express.", "A shut_in at high, which is the type reserved for a well that produced in the baseline window and nothing in the test window.", "Nothing, because windowMean returns a null mean and every comparison on that well is skipped rather than reported as zero.", "A downtime at medium, because an empty window averages zero hours."]'::jsonb, 2, 'windowMean skips values that are not finite and returns a null mean with a count of zero, and a comparison against a null is not made at all, so the well is silent rather than flagged.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-two-windows-on-one-well', 12, 'OGUTA-2 has a recent oil mean of 618.142857142857 stb over 7 rows and a baseline of 1038.900000000000 stb over 30 rows. What does the returned exception say about those two counts?', '["It reports both counts beside the value and the baseline, which is how a reader tells a full window from one with holes in the ledger.", "Nothing at all, because the count that windowMean carried is not in the returned row.", "It reports the recent count only, since the baseline is required to be complete before a comparison is allowed to run.", "It reports the ratio of the two counts as a coverage figure, so a window averaged over fewer rows is visibly weaker."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A window is a date range and not a row count, so a well with holes in its ledger averages fewer rows over the same dates and the exception row cannot be told apart from a complete one.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-two-windows-on-one-well', 13, 'OGUTA-2''s recent window holds 4327.000000000 stb of oil and 2792.000000000 Mscf of gas over 7 rows. Read as a mean of daily ratios its gas-oil ratio is 1066.663410762250 scf/stb and read volumetrically it is 645.250751097758 scf/stb. Which does detectExceptions compare?', '["The volumetric reading, since a period ratio is the sum of gas over the sum of oil and that is what a window mean of a period means.", "Whichever is larger, so a rise is never understated.", "Both, and it prints the pair so the reader can see which reading produced the severity beside them.", "The mean of daily ratios, off the per-row gor key that derivePoint formed from each row alone."]'::jsonb, 3, 'windowMean is an unweighted arithmetic mean of the values it finds, which on a ratio makes a low-rate day speak as loudly as a full one, and computeKpis in the same file forms the same ratio volumetrically.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-two-windows-on-one-well', 14, 'Over the OGUTA-2 baseline window the two readings of the gas-oil ratio agree exactly at 580.000000000000 scf/stb, a difference of 0.000000000000. What does that window have that the recent one does not?', '["Thirty rows on which the daily ratio never moves, and a window of uniform days cannot show the disagreement at all.", "A volumetric reading only, because the baseline is formed by computeKpis and the recent window by detectExceptions.", "More rows, and the gap between the two readings shrinks with the square root of the count in a window.", "A gas column with no nulls in it, since a skipped row is what makes the two readings part company."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The two readings diverge in proportion to how much of a window a low-rate day is allowed to speak for, which is why a seam this size survived into a shipped studio.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-two-windows-on-one-well', 15, 'OGUTA-6 has a mean calendar oil of 307.504761904762 stb over the recent window against 502.666666666667 stb over the baseline, and a mean producing-day oil rate of 512.000000000000 stb/d against 502.666666666667 stb/d. Which column produced the reported 38.825312618416 per cent?', '["The producing-day rate, the column the header calls the one that says how the well is performing.", "Both, since the two agree whenever a well''s hours on stream are steady.", "The calendar volume, and the producing-day rate over the same rows went up by 1.856763925729 per cent.", "The liquid column, oil plus water, reported under the oil label."]'::jsonb, 2, 'detectExceptions sets its rate key to the calendar volume, so the volume fell and the rate did not, and the exception that would have named the cause does not fire either at 14.414285714286 h against a threshold of 12.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-seven-exceptions', 1, 'detectExceptions sets its rate key by well type. Which column does a producer''s rate_drop compare?', '["The producing-day oil rate in stb/d, which is the column the file computes on every point as oilPd.", "The calendar oil volume off the ledger row, stb over a row, and never the producing-day rate.", "The liquid volume, oil plus water, so that a well cutting water is not reported as a faller.", "Whichever of the two the row carries, with the producing-day rate preferred when an hours column is present."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The key is oil for a producer and winj for an injector, both calendar volumes, and the message that prints them hard-codes stb/d after a number that is a volume over a row.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-seven-exceptions', 2, 'On the published field, P-2 raises shut_in at high with a value of 0.000000000000 against a baseline of 400.000000000000 while P-1 raises rate_drop at medium at 555.714285714286 against 900.000000000000. Why does the smaller well outrank the larger one?', '["A fall to zero is an infinite percentage drop, so it crosses the doubled rateDropPct trigger by an unbounded margin.", "P-2''s baseline sits closer to minOilRate, and a rate drop measured on a small baseline is scored more severely to compensate.", "The shut_in check runs before the rate check, and the first type to fire on a well takes the higher of the two available severities.", "shut_in is high whatever the baseline was, so its severity is not earned by a size at all."]'::jsonb, 3, 'No size of drop can produce a shut_in and no smallness of one can soften it, which is why the type sits outside the doubling family entirely.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-seven-exceptions', 3, 'A well''s mean hours on stream over the recent window are swept from 24.00 down to 0.00. At which values does downtime fire, and at what severity?', '["At every value below 12 and above zero, always at medium, so a well averaging 0.10 h and a well averaging 11.99 h are ranked identically.", "Below 12 h at medium and below 6 h at high, the doubling rule that four of the seven types use.", "At every value below 12 h including 0.00, always at medium.", "Only below 12 h on a well that also raises a rate drop, since the two checks share the minOilRate gate."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The test is mean < downtimeHours and mean > 0, with no second threshold and no path to high, so the check has one rank and one edge.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-seven-exceptions', 4, 'A well recorded itself shut for the whole recent window, giving a mean hours on stream of exactly 0.00. What does the downtime check report?', '["downtime at medium, since 0.00 is below the 12 h threshold like every other value in the sweep.", "downtime at high, because a full window at zero hours is read as the largest downtime there is and so doubles the 12 h trigger.", "Nothing, because the mean > 0 clause that was written to keep an absent hours column out takes a real zero with it.", "shut_in at high, because the downtime branch hands a fully shut well to the type that names a stop."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The one value an operator most wants reported is the one value the check refuses, and at 0.10 h the same well raises a medium downtime.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-seven-exceptions', 5, 'Against a staleDays of 7, what severity does a well silent for 400 days carry?', '["high, because doubling the staleDays trigger raises stale_data the way it raises a rate drop.", "info, because the doubling applies only to the four types that compare two windows.", "It carries no severity, because a gap that long empties both windows and the comparison is skipped.", "medium, the same rank as a well silent for 15 days."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Doubling staleDays takes stale_data from info to medium and no further, so the row that says the data has stopped arriving is ordered among the mediums alphabetically.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-seven-exceptions', 6, 'stale_data fires on a well. What happens to the rest of the checks on that well?', '["They run as usual, and any exception they raise is appended after the stale row in the same list.", "None of them runs, because the type returns early and no other comparison on that well is ever made.", "They run but their severities are capped at info, so a stale well cannot contribute a high row to the field list.", "They run on the baseline window only, since the recent window is the one the silence emptied."]'::jsonb, 1, 'OGUTA-17 raises stale_data at medium, value = 23.000000000000 against a baseline of 7.000000000000, and nothing else, though its ledger simply stops while the facility keeps producing it.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-seven-exceptions', 7, 'OGUTA-5 has a baseline oil mean of 3.600000000000 stb, below the minOilRate default of 5. What does it still raise?', '["Nothing, because minOilRate gates every comparison on a well whose baseline mean is below it.", "gor_rise only, since a gas-oil ratio is the one ratio that stays meaningful at a rate the rate check will not report.", "watercut_rise at high, value = 0.867469879518 against a baseline of 0.280000000000, because minOilRate does not gate the watercut check.", "rate_drop at medium, because a fall from a small baseline still clears the 20 per cent trigger."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The gate sits on the rate check and the gas-oil ratio check and not on the watercut check, so a well too small to have a collapse reported still raises a high watercut on the same rows.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-seven-exceptions', 8, 'Setting minOilRate to 0 on the teaching field takes the list from 8 exceptions to 9. Which row appears?', '["A high rate_drop on OGUTA-5, the well whose baseline oil mean of 3.600000000000 stb sits under the default gate.", "A medium downtime on OGUTA-6, whose mean recent hours are 14.414285714286 h.", "A second stale_data row on OGUTA-17, this time at info.", "A gor_rise on OGUTA-9, whose recent oil mean is 942.836455279584 stb."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The small well''s watercut_rise was already in the list at the default of 5, and lowering the gate to 0 lets its rate comparison be reported as well.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-seven-exceptions', 9, 'watercutRisePts defaults to 10 and gorRisePct defaults to 30. What is the difference between the two triggers?', '["One is a fraction and the other is a percentage of the same underlying quantity, so a rise of 10 and a rise of 30 are the same size of move on identical rows.", "One doubles to high and the other has a single rank, which is why the watercut trigger is set at the smaller of the two numbers.", "One is measured on the recent window and the other on the difference between the two windows.", "One is measured in POINTS of a 0 to 1 watercut fraction and the other in PER CENT of a gas-oil ratio in scf/stb."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A watercut in surveillance is a 0 to 1 fraction and its rise is in points, doubling to high at 20; a gas-oil ratio rise is a percentage, doubling to high at 60.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-seven-exceptions', 10, 'On the published well P-1 the gas-oil ratio rise reads 70.033482142857 per cent as a mean of daily ratios and 42.737789203085 per cent volumetrically. What does the printed exception show?', '["medium, because the engine takes the volumetric reading, the sum of gas over the sum of oil, which is what a period ratio means.", "high, because detectExceptions forms the ratio as a mean of daily ratios and the other reading would have printed medium.", "high, because both readings clear the doubled 60 per cent trigger and the two agree on the rank.", "Two rows, one at each severity, since the golden publishes the disagreement rather than resolving it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Neither reading is wrong: one answers what a typical day looked like and the other what the period produced, and the golden records the seam as a measurement, which is the right thing to have done.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-seven-exceptions', 11, 'On the teaching well OGUTA-2 the gas-oil ratio rise is 83.907484614181 per cent read as a mean of daily ratios and 11.250129499613 per cent read volumetrically off the same seven recent rows. What does the choice of reading decide there?', '["Whether the well appears in the list at all, since the first reading is a high exception and the second raises nothing.", "Whether the row prints high or medium, which is the same move the seam makes on the published well.", "Whether the row is a gor_rise or a watercut_rise, because the two ratios swap ranks under the volumetric reading.", "Nothing, because the printed severity is set by the rate check on the same well."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The trigger is 30 per cent with a doubling to high at 60, so 83.907484614181 per cent is high and 11.250129499613 per cent is under the trigger entirely.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-seven-exceptions', 12, 'The teaching list comes back with OGUTA-14 first and OGUTA-2 second, both rate_drop at high. What decided that order?', '["The size of the fall, 11600.000000000000 against a baseline of 24400.000000000000 being the larger of the two.", "The volume involved, since the sort ranks by the barrels the exception puts at risk.", "The well name, because ties within a severity break on a string comparison.", "The order the wells appear in the ledger handed to the function."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The sort key is severity, then well name, with severity ranks of high 0, medium 1 and info 2, so nothing in the ordering reflects how much oil is involved.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-seven-exceptions', 13, 'The teaching field holds 8 wells and 7 were surveilled. OGUTA-21 and OGUTA-9 both appear in no exception. What is the difference between the two silences?', '["OGUTA-21 raised an info row that the default severity filter dropped before the list was sorted, while OGUTA-9 raised nothing at any rank.", "OGUTA-21 was filtered out as an observation well before any comparison ran, while OGUTA-9 was compared and found quiet.", "OGUTA-21 has no baseline window, while OGUTA-9 has no recent window.", "OGUTA-21 was carried by a test that aged out of its window, while OGUTA-9 was never handed to the function at all."]'::jsonb, 1, 'OGUTA-9 has a recent mean calendar oil of 942.836455279584 stb against a baseline of 1004.457323679256 stb, a change of -6.134742308 per cent, under every trigger, and the list shows neither reason.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-seven-exceptions', 14, 'A well is filed with a type of gas_injector. How does detectExceptions read it?', '["As an injector, compared on its winj column, since the branch matches any type containing the word injector.", "It is filtered out before any comparison runs, the way an observation well is.", "It is compared on both columns and the larger of the two changes is reported.", "As a producer, compared on its oil column, because the injector branch matches the exact string injector."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The filter is on the exact type observation and the rate key is chosen by a branch on the exact type injector, so every other spelling takes the producer path whatever the person filing the well record meant.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-seven-exceptions', 15, 'Raising rateDropPct from 20 to a stricter setting on the published field takes the list from 9 exceptions to 6. What happened to the P-5 rate drop, which stays at value = 100.000000000000 against a baseline of 300.000000000000?', '["It is still there and now prints at medium rather than high, on rows that did not change.", "It is gone, because a stricter trigger removes the rate drops before it changes any severity.", "It is unchanged at high, because its fall of 67 per cent clears any trigger the setting can take.", "It is now reported as a shut_in, since a stricter rate trigger hands the largest falls to the type that names a stop."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The P-1 rate drop is the row that disappears, and no exception carries the setting that put it there, so a list of 9 and a list of 6 on identical data are not distinguishable from the rows alone.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-test-that-carries-a-well', 1, 'On well w-p1 the published probes return t-p1-a on 2025-02-04 and t-p1-b on 2025-02-05. What is the rule?', '["The latest test dated on or before the day, provided its age is inside maxTestAgeDays, so a newer test takes over on its own date and not before it.", "The nearest test in either direction, so a test filed two days later is preferred to one filed a month earlier.", "An interpolation between the two tests either side of the day, weighted by the distance to each.", "The most recent test on the well whatever its date, with the age limit applied only to the allocation total."]'::jsonb, 0, 'There is no blending and no extrapolation of a decline between two tests, so the rate steps on the date of the newer test and holds flat until the next one.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-test-that-carries-a-well', 2, 'Published well w-p3 returns no test in force at all six probe dates, at both 180 and 120 days. What happens to that well in the allocation?', '["It takes a share computed from the field average rate, which is what the diagnostic is warning about.", "It takes a zero share, so it appears in the return with an allocated volume of nothing.", "It takes no share of the metered stream at all and records a no_test_in_force diagnostic.", "It takes a share on its own ledger rows instead, since the basis falls back from test to ledger for that well."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Over the teaching field''s 21 allocated days only four wells of eight ever take a share and the run records 57 no_test_in_force diagnostics.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-test-that-carries-a-well', 3, 'OGUTA-17 is carried on 2024-11-05 by test g-o17-1 dated 2024-05-09, which is 180 days old that day, and has no test in force on 2024-11-06. What does that show about the limit?', '["It is exclusive, so a test at exactly 180 days has already aged out and the well was carried by a different test on 2024-11-05.", "It is inclusive, so a test exactly at maxTestAgeDays still carries its well and one day past it the well drops out.", "It is measured from the allocated window''s first date rather than from each date in turn.", "It is applied to the well rather than the test, so a well with any test inside the limit is carried on all of them."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The same well also has a test dated 2024-11-06 at 421.000000 stb/d, and that one was filed with is_valid false, so nothing replaces the carrier that just expired.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-test-that-carries-a-well', 4, 'A demonstration test 2088 days old is probed on 2024-11-20 at a maxTestAgeDays of 0. What comes back?', '["Nothing, because 0 means no age is acceptable and every test on file is older than that.", "Nothing, because a setting of 0 is replaced by the default parameter of 180 and the test is well past that.", "An error, because the function refuses a limit that no test can satisfy.", "The test, because the guard is Number.isFinite(x) and x > 0, and 0 fails it and turns the age check off."]'::jsonb, 3, 'At 180 and at 365 the same probe returns nothing, and 0, -1, NaN, null and Infinity all return the 2088 day old test, so the most permissive setting on the list is the one that reads as the strictest.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-test-that-carries-a-well', 5, 'maxTestAgeDays is omitted entirely on one call and passed as null on the next. What is the difference?', '["No difference, since both spellings of an absent value take the default parameter of 180.", "No difference, since both fail the finite check and turn the age check off.", "The omitted call returns no test in force and the null call returns the 2088 day old one.", "The omitted call throws, and the null call takes the default of 180 and refuses the old test."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A missing argument takes the default parameter of 180 while null skips the guard, and both are how a form field spells the user did not fill this in.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-test-that-carries-a-well', 6, 'On the teaching field, maxTestAgeDays of 60 gives 2 wells with a share and a theoretical oil of 39172.000000000 stb, while 0 gives 4 wells and 65033.920833333 stb. Why does the tighter looking setting admit fewer wells than 0?', '["Because 60 is a working limit that throws two carriers out, and 0 is not a limit at all, so tests the default had already discarded carry their wells again.", "Because 0 is read as no limit on the number of wells rather than on the age of a test.", "Because a theoretical of zero on a well has no factor, so the run at 60 loses those wells to a no_basis instead.", "Because the metered oil moves between the two runs and fewer barrels support fewer wells."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The allocated oil is 58426.784897363 stb in both runs and in every other row of that sweep, so nothing about the meter changed and the whole move is on the theoretical side.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-test-that-carries-a-well', 7, 'At the default the teaching field''s last-day oil factor is 1.190463125091, and at a maxTestAgeDays of 0 it is 0.909283141463. What does the second number tell you about the run?', '["That the tests agree with the meter better once the stale ones are excluded from the split.", "That the meter is reading closer to the wells, since a factor is a property of the facility measurement.", "That two wells were removed from the split, which is what pulled the factor down towards one.", "Nothing good, because it is the run with the age check disabled and a factor nearer one is not evidence that a setting is sensible."]'::jsonb, 3, 'It is also the row with the fewest no-test diagnostics, 42 against 57, so the dial that looks healthiest on both readouts is the one that turned a guard off.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-test-that-carries-a-well', 8, 'groupTests is handed six tests whose is_valid values are true, absent, null, the string false, a zero and a boolean false. How many does it keep at the default settings?', '["One, the test whose flag is true, since every other spelling fails the check.", "Five, because a test is dropped only when the flag is STRICTLY false.", "Three, since a null, an absent flag and a zero are all falsy and are dropped with the boolean.", "Six, because groupTests reads only the well and the date and leaves validity to the QC."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A test that was never QCd, a null, a zero and the string false are all candidates, so the one gate on content is a flag that whoever filed the test set by hand.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-test-that-carries-a-well', 9, 'Setting includeInvalidTests to true on the teaching field''s 21 days moves the theoretical oil from 54713.920833333 stb to 61028.920833333 stb. What else moves?', '["The allocated oil, which rises with the theoretical because every share is a theoretical times a factor.", "The measured oil, since a test that was refused had also been kept out of the metered total for those days.", "The last-day oil factor, from 1.190463125091 to 1.001040970439, and the no_test_in_force count from 57 to 42.", "Nothing else, because the setting changes which tests are candidates and not what any of them is worth."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A factor of 1.001040970439 looks like the tests agreeing with the meter, and it was produced by putting the refused tests back in.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-test-that-carries-a-well', 10, 'Which pair of QC codes needs a ledger row on the test date to be evaluated at all?', '["rate_outlier and ledger_mismatch, both of which compare the test against a second reading of the same well.", "zero_rate and short_duration, since a test with no flow and a test too short to stabilize are both judged against the ledger.", "watercut_mismatch and rate_outlier, since a watercut and a median both need a history the ledger holds.", "ledger_mismatch and watercut_mismatch."]'::jsonb, 3, 'No ledger row means neither comparison runs and a no_ledger code at info is all that is reported, so their silence says nothing about the test.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-test-that-carries-a-well', 11, 'A well''s tests run at 500 stb/d and the last one reads 1500 stb/d. At what point does rate_outlier first fire?', '["When the well has four tests, so that three prior ones exist for the median.", "On the second test, as soon as one prior test exists to compare against.", "On the third test, when a median of two prior tests can be formed.", "Never, because the median is taken over every test on the well including the one being judged."]'::jsonb, 0, 'With two and with three tests on the well the last test comes back with no_ledger alone, and the outlierPct default of 50 is never even consulted.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-test-that-carries-a-well', 12, 'Why is a well''s first bad test worse than a later one for the outlier check?', '["It is caught twice, once as an outlier and once as a ledger mismatch, so it inflates the well''s issue count.", "It is never caught, and it then joins the median that judges every test after it.", "It is compared against the field median instead of the well''s, which is a weaker comparison.", "It is dropped from the history, so the median stays narrow."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The median is taken over the EARLIER tests on the same well only, so a bad first reading is admitted and then defended by its own presence in the history.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-test-that-carries-a-well', 13, 'The published QC hands in 7 tests and 4 rows come back. What do the other 3 mean?', '["They were dropped before the QC ran, because their is_valid flag was strictly false.", "They were checked and passed every code, and the return records them with an empty code list.", "They had no ledger row on their test date, so no check could run and they were skipped.", "They had no issue at all, and there is no count of tests checked anywhere in the return."]'::jsonb, 3, 'QC on an empty test list returns 0 rows as well, so an empty array means either every test was clean or nothing ran and a caller cannot tell which.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-test-that-carries-a-well', 14, 'Teaching test g-o9-1 comes out of QC at medium with a watercut_mismatch. What does that do to the allocation?', '["It excludes the test, so OGUTA-9 takes no share on the days that test would have carried.", "It halves the well''s theoretical, which is how a medium QC severity is carried into the split.", "Nothing, because groupTests reads is_valid and never asks the QC anything.", "It raises a no_test_in_force diagnostic on every date the test would have been in force."]'::jsonb, 2, 'That test carries OGUTA-9 through the whole allocated window, 21 days on a theoretical oil of 19488.000000000 stb and an allocated oil of 20995.477445353 stb.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-test-that-carries-a-well', 15, 'Test g-o17-2 is the one test on the teaching field that the split does drop. What dropped it?', '["The is_valid flag on the row, which whoever filed the test set to false.", "Its QC verdict, a short_duration at medium on a 2.5 h test, which the split reads before grouping.", "Its age, since the test is outside maxTestAgeDays on every date in the allocated window.", "The absence of a ledger row on its date, which leaves it with no basis to be checked against."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The QC and the grouping live in one file and neither asks the other anything, so a test the same file calls high on three codes can go on carrying its well at full weight.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-one-meter-many-wells', 1, 'computeAllocation forms three quantities in order for each date and phase. What are they?', '["A share per well from its ledger row, then a factor to bring the shares up to the meter, then a theoretical as a check on the pair.", "A factor per well from its own test against its own ledger, then a theoretical, then a share as the mean of the two.", "A theoretical per well, then a factor as the metered total over the sum of the theoreticals, then each share as that factor times that well''s theoretical.", "A metered total per well, then a theoretical from the test in force, then a residual booked against the field."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The theoretical is a test rate in stb/d times an uptime fraction, which gives a volume in stb over that date, and the factor is dimensionless.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-one-meter-many-wells', 2, 'On 2024-11-20 OGUTA-2 reads 1014.000000000 stb in its own ledger row, 1036.000000000 stb as a theoretical and 1233.319797595 stb as an allocated share. Which of the three did a meter see?', '["None of them, because the meter measured the field stream and the split divided that one number across a set of models.", "The ledger row, which is the well''s own measurement, taken off the well''s own instrumentation, and the only unmodelled number of the three.", "The allocated share, since it is the only one of the three that carries the metered total into the well.", "The theoretical, because it comes from a test in which the well was measured on its own."]'::jsonb, 0, 'All three are calendar volumes over one date, and naming which one is being quoted is the whole discipline, since an allocated volume carries a field-wide correction and a theoretical is a test rate scaled by an uptime.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-one-meter-many-wells', 3, 'The oil factor on the teaching field''s last allocated day is 1.190463125091. Whose property is it?', '["The best-tested well''s, because the factor is anchored on the well whose test is freshest and the rest of the field is then scaled to that well''s agreement with the meter.", "The meter''s, because a factor above one is what a facility meter reading high looks like.", "Each well''s own, since every well is scaled by the ratio of its own allocated volume to its own theoretical volume for the date it was allocated on.", "The field''s, since it is the ratio between what the meter saw, 2648.616764649 stb, and what the models claimed, 2224.862500000 stb."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every well on the date is multiplied by that same number, so OGUTA-6 goes from a theoretical of 260.862500000 stb to an allocated 310.547186969 stb and an error anywhere in the set arrives in every well.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-one-meter-many-wells', 4, 'Turning useUptime off on the published 24 days moves the theoretical oil from 38046.666666667 stb to 41300.000000000 stb and the grand factor from 1.043350271596 to 0.961162227603. What does the measured oil do?', '["It rises with the theoretical, since a full day on stream credits every well with more volume.", "Nothing, because it stays at 39696.000000000 stb and so does the allocated oil.", "It falls, because the metered total is rescaled by the new factor before the shares are written.", "It is not returned in that run, since a run without uptimes has no basis to measure against."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The five published runs are the same field and the same meter under five settings, and only the theoretical side moves, so a factor swinging from 0.961162227603 to 1.686321155480 involves no metered barrel changing.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-one-meter-many-wells', 5, 'A two-well demonstration is told that SHARE-B was shut in, with an hours_on of 0, and the oil factor is 1.000000000000 with SHARE-A allocated 1000.000000 stb. The same demonstration is then told nothing about SHARE-B at all. What happens?', '["The factor is unchanged, because a well with no ledger row also has no theoretical and cannot be in the sum.", "SHARE-B is dropped with a no_test_in_force diagnostic and SHARE-A keeps all 1000.000000 stb.", "The run refuses the date, because a well in the well list with no row for the date leaves the sum incomplete.", "The oil factor is 0.500000000000 and SHARE-A is allocated 500.000000 stb, with the other half going to a well that produced nothing."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The lookup returns null, Number.isFinite(null?.hours_on) is false, and the defaultHours of 24 is substituted, so a well that filed nothing is credited with a full day on stream.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-one-meter-many-wells', 6, 'SHARE-B files a row of zeroes with an hours_on of 24, then a row with a null hours_on, then no row at all. What do the three runs give?', '["Three different factors, because a null hours_on is read as unknown, a zero row as a real zero and an absent row as no well on the date at all.", "A factor of 1.000000000000 in all three, since none of the three rows carries a produced volume for a theoretical to be built out of.", "The same oil factor of 0.500000000000 in all three, so the module cannot tell an idle well from an absent one.", "A factor of 0.500000000000 on the row of zeroes only, the other two raising a no_basis instead."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Only the run that was told the truth, an hours_on of 0, gives a factor of 1.000000000000 and hands SHARE-A every barrel it made.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-one-meter-many-wells', 7, 'Why does an allocated day close exactly, with a published closure residual of 0.000000000000?', '["Because every share on the date is the same factor times a theoretical, so the shares sum to the factor times the sum of the theoreticals, which is the metered total the factor was built from.", "Because the last well on the date absorbs whatever the earlier shares left over.", "Because the shares are rounded to the metered total after the split is written.", "Because the tests were validated against the ledger before the split ran."]'::jsonb, 0, 'It is an algebraic identity that would hold just as exactly if every test on the field were wrong.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-one-meter-many-wells', 8, 'On 2024-11-20 OGUTA-2 is credited with 1233.319797595 stb of allocated oil against its own ledger row of 1014.000000000 stb, and the day closes to zero. What does the closure prove?', '["That the metered total was correctly apportioned, since a mismatch of that size could not survive a closure residual of exactly zero.", "That one factor multiplied every share on the date, and nothing about whether a well made what it was credited with.", "That the well''s own ledger row is wrong, because the allocated volume is the one of the two that was reconciled against the facility meter.", "That the uptime used for the well was right, since an error in the hours would move the theoretical and break the identity."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Closure is a consequence of multiplying by one factor and is not evidence that the tests, the uptimes or the meter are right.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-one-meter-many-wells', 9, 'The published noBasis case runs two days with 830.000000000 stb of measured oil and 520.000000000 stb of allocated oil. Where did the other 310.000000000 stb go?', '["Onto the following date, whose factor absorbs the volume the earlier date could not place.", "Into a field residual line on the grand object, which is what the no_basis diagnostic counts once for each phase on the date.", "Nowhere, because the second date has no factor and its metered volume is in no well and in no total.", "It was spread across the wells in proportion to their theoreticals on the day they last had one."]'::jsonb, 2, 'That date carries null factors for oil, water and gas on a theoretical oil of 0.000000000 and an allocated oil of 0.000000000, and the diagnostics are a count of 3 no_basis.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-one-meter-many-wells', 10, 'A consumer wants to know whether a whole allocation run closed. What does the return give it?', '["A closure residual on the grand object, computed as the per-day rows compute theirs and printed beside the measured and allocated totals.", "A no_basis count, which the return states as the number of barrels that fell out of the grand allocated on the dates that had no factor.", "A factor of one on the grand object whenever the run closed.", "Nothing directly, since the keys are days, diagnosticCounts, grand, settings and wells and it has to do the subtraction itself."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The teaching run closes at 58426.784897363 stb measured against 58426.784897363 stb allocated, and nothing in the object prompts a reader to check.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-one-meter-many-wells', 11, 'Four of the five published allocation runs return an allocated oil equal to the measured 39696.000000000 stb. The allocationAged120 run returns 36372.000000000 stb. What is different about it?', '["It has dates with no basis, 6 of them, and the metered volume on such a date is lost from the grand allocated rather than shared out among the wells.", "Its factor of 1.686321155480 is outside the warning band, so the shares it produced were clamped back.", "It allocated to a single well, and one well cannot absorb the whole metered stream.", "Its tests aged out mid-window, so the last dates were allocated on the ledger basis instead."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Its allocated water of 10673.000000000 stb against a measured 11604.000000000 stb is the same loss on another phase, and the only trace is a diagnostic count.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-one-meter-many-wells', 12, 'A monthly factor comes back as exactly 1.000000000000 on the gas phase. What are the two things it can mean?', '["That the phase was not metered for that month, or that the well was off for every date the split covered.", "That the tests agreed with the meter exactly, or that there was no theoretical gas volume to scale.", "That the factor was clamped into the warning band, or that the month held a single date.", "That the allocated gas equals the metered gas, or that the well had no test in force."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The demonstration well whose test recorded no gas returns a gas factor of 1.000000000000 on a metered gas of 500.000000 Mscf, a theoretical gas of 0.000000 Mscf and an allocated gas of 0.000000 Mscf, and the diagnostic that tells the two apart is on a different array.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-one-meter-many-wells', 13, 'On the teaching field all four wells carry an October oil factor of 0.947703084569 while their November factors differ. Why?', '["October was allocated on the ledger basis and November on the test basis, so the two months carry factors of different construction.", "October had no well out of the split, so the monthly factor reported is the field''s grand factor rather than each well''s own.", "October''s factor was inside the warning band set by factorWarnLow and factorWarnHigh and November''s was not, so only November''s was recomputed.", "October holds one allocated date, so every well carries that date''s factor, and November is a month of mixed days."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A monthly factor is one well''s allocated volume over its own theoretical volume for the month, so it carries the mix of days that well was actually in the split.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-one-meter-many-wells', 14, 'allocatedLedgerRows writes 69 rows across 4 teaching wells and every one carries an hours_on, 24.000000000 for OGUTA-2 and 12.300000000 for OGUTA-6. What is wrong with the 24?', '["It can be the value the split substituted for a well that filed no row, so a silent well acquires a row saying it ran all day.", "It is a rate rather than a duration, since the write-back carries the test rate through unchanged.", "It is the field default rather than the well''s, so every written row carries the same figure.", "It is unmarked as allocated rather than measured, which is the only thing separating a written row from one the well filed for itself that day."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Read back by derivePoint an absent hours column is uptime unknown, and read back from a written ledger the same absence has become a full day on stream.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-one-meter-many-wells', 15, 'The teaching oil factor is 1.216888186094 on 2024-11-15 and 0.790840256747 on 2024-11-16, with 3 entries on both dates and no test changing. What moved it?', '["The meter, which is the only measured reading in the run and therefore the only one a factor can be describing when it moves by a third overnight.", "A well leaving the split when its test aged out, which is what took an earlier date on this field from 4 entries to 3 and moved the factor then.", "One well, OGUTA-2, whose own ledger row went from 1021.000000 stb to 88.000000 stb while its test still claimed a full day.", "The uptime column, since a factor is the sum of the uptimes over the number of wells."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Because every share is that same factor times a theoretical, OGUTA-6 and OGUTA-9 were scaled down on 2024-11-16 for something that happened on a different well.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-what-the-meter-and-the-model-say', 1, 'imbalanceSeries compares the facility meter against what the wells booked in their own ledger rows. What does it never look at?', '["The well tests, so its percentage moves whenever a test ages out of force and a well leaves the split.", "The allocation, which is why it is the one number in the module that a split cannot flatter.", "The uptime column, so a well on a short day is booked as a full one.", "The gas phase, which is reported as a volume with no percentage beside it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The percentage is the metered volume minus the booked volume over the booked volume, and every quantity in it is a calendar volume over one date.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-what-the-meter-and-the-model-say', 2, 'On the teaching field the oil imbalance is 423.638308060 stb at 31.931380703618 per cent on 2024-11-17 and 443.885833337 stb at 20.396841769205 per cent on 2024-11-18. Which day has more unaccounted oil?', '["2024-11-17, because a higher percentage of a common denominator is more barrels.", "Neither, because the two percentages are of different metered totals and cannot be ordered.", "2024-11-17, because the imbalance is expressed as a share of the meter and the meter did not move between the two dates.", "2024-11-18, even though its percentage is about eleven points lower."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The denominator is what the wells booked, and the wells that were booking it collapsed, so the percentage rises when the numerator falls.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-what-the-meter-and-the-model-say', 3, 'A date arrives on which the facility meter saw volume and no well filed a ledger row at all. What does imbalanceSeries return for it?', '["A real imbalance in stb and a null percentage, with nothing in the row saying which well went quiet.", "A percentage of 100, since the whole metered volume is unaccounted for once no well has booked anything against it.", "No row for that date, because a comparison needs both sides.", "A percentage against the previous date''s booked volume."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The percentage is taken against what the wells booked, so a booked volume of nothing leaves the ratio undefined and the stb column standing on its own.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-what-the-meter-and-the-model-say', 4, 'On the published date 2025-01-25 the oil imbalance is 5.000000000 stb at 0.294985250737 per cent and the water imbalance is -188.333333000 stb at -33.137829872808 per cent. What does that pair say?', '["That the water meter is broken, since a third of the water is unaccounted for on a day the oil closes to within a third of a per cent.", "That the oil figure is the reliable one, because a percentage that small is a measurement error and a percentage that large is a real loss.", "That the phases are separate comparisons and a field can be nearly closed on one and badly out on another.", "That the two are the same discrepancy expressed twice, once in stb of oil and once in stb of water."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Each phase takes its own metered total against its own booked total, and the same date carries a gas imbalance of 83.333333000 Mscf at 10.460250999807 per cent as well.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-what-the-meter-and-the-model-say', 5, 'The published nodal check reports n-low at -54.236118203180 per cent and n-high at 44.918959023262 per cent against the same nodal rate of 1311.077593164 stb/d. Why are the two percentages not symmetric about the gaps that produced them?', '["Because the tolerancePct of 35 is applied to the low side before the percentage is formed.", "The deviation divides by the NODAL rate, so it says how far the test sits from the model rather than how far the model sits from the test.", "Because a rate below the model is compared against the well model''s own water cut and a rate above it is not.", "Because the gaps are not equal: n-low is -711.077593164 stb/d from the model and n-high is 588.922406836 stb/d from it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Divide the same two gaps by the measured rate instead and neither printed percentage is the one you get, and nothing in the returned row names the denominator.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-what-the-meter-and-the-model-say', 6, 'Three of the six published nodal tests come back with a null nodal rate and a null deviation. What has been established about those three?', '["Nothing about the tests, because each carries a status naming what was missing rather than a verdict.", "That they passed, since a deviation that is not outside the tolerance is not reported.", "That they failed, since a test the model could not reproduce at all is a test that disagrees with its own well.", "That they were below minRateStbd, which is the one condition that suppresses a deviation."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The three statuses are dead, no-thp and no-model: a model saying the well should not flow, a missing tubing head pressure and a missing well model.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-what-the-meter-and-the-model-say', 7, 'The nodal check needs a water cut and a gas-oil ratio for the solver. What does each fall back to when the test recorded no liquid or no gas?', '["Both fall back to the well model''s own values, which is the reason the check needs a well model in the first place.", "Both fall back to zero, so a test with either column missing is solved as dead oil against a well the module treats as making no water.", "The water cut falls back to zero and the gas-oil ratio to the model''s, which is the safer pairing of the two.", "The water cut falls back to the well model''s own and the gas-oil ratio on the very next line falls back to zero."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A test that recorded a rate and no gas is therefore solved against a well the module has just decided makes no gas, and the deviation is measured against the same tolerancePct of 35 as any other.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-what-the-meter-and-the-model-say', 8, 'summarizeDeferments is the one function in surveillance.js that can give a different answer tomorrow on the same data. What makes that true?', '["It reads the ledger''s latest date for its anchor, which moves whenever a late row arrives after a run was already made.", "It sorts by oil descending and then by days, so two categories carrying similar volumes can swap places between one run and the next.", "An omitted asOf is replaced with today''s date, and an open event accrues its days to whatever the asOf is.", "It clamps its day count at one, which hides the direction of an event whose dates were filed the wrong way round."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Anchored at 2024-11-20 the open event is 173 days and anchored at 2025-06-30 it is 395 days, and its oil is 3200.000000 stb in both, because only the days move.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-what-the-meter-and-the-model-say', 9, 'A deferment event is filed running 2024-06-10 to 2024-05-10, an end date a month before its start. What does the roll-up report?', '["1 day, silently, because the count is clamped at one and nothing says the dates were the wrong way round.", "31 days, the absolute value of the interval, since the count is formed from the size of the gap between the two dates.", "0 days, since an event that ends before it starts has no duration to book and the roll-up floors a negative count at zero.", "It is refused and left out of the roll-up entirely."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The count is the end day minus the start day plus one with a floor of 1, so a same-day event and a reversed one are indistinguishable in the return.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-what-the-meter-and-the-model-say', 10, 'The published deferment roll-up puts Well integrity first with 1 event, 10 days and 5100.000000 stb of oil, above Facility with 2 events, 6 days and 4600.000000 stb. What is the sort?', '["Event count descending, with oil as the tie break, so a category with more events leads.", "Oil descending and then days, which is the right order for a loss review and the wrong one for a maintenance backlog.", "Days descending, since a longer outage is the more serious one whatever it cost.", "Alphabetical by category, because no ordering by size would put a single event first."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Facility has twice the events of Well integrity and sits below it, and 5 events over 21 days and 10850.000000 stb of oil is the whole book.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-what-the-meter-and-the-model-say', 11, 'The teaching deferments hold 12800.000000 stb of oil over the whole ledger and the field oil at a 7 day window is 1869.584074327 stb/d. Why does the sentence saying the book is worth 6.846442573 days of production need care?', '["Because the deferment book covers 29 days and the window covers 7, so the two spans do not line up.", "Because the deferred oil is a modelled loss and the field oil is metered, so the two are not comparable at all.", "Because the field figure is a mean over a window and would be a different number over the baseline window instead.", "Because one of the two is a volume and the other is a rate, and the sentence only works if it says which is which."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The deferred oil is a filed volume in stb and the field figure is a rate in stb/d, and dividing one by the other is what produces a number of days.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-what-the-meter-and-the-model-say', 12, 'An allocation returns factor_out_of_band, no_test_in_force and no_basis. Which of the three changes the split?', '["All three, because each one names a condition that the split then corrects for before the shares are written.", "factor_out_of_band only, since a factor outside the warning band is pulled back inside it before the shares are multiplied.", "no_test_in_force and no_basis, since one takes a well out and the other takes a date out.", "None of them, because a diagnostic is a report rather than an instruction."]'::jsonb, 2, 'factor_out_of_band fires when a factor falls outside factorWarnLow of 0.7 or above factorWarnHigh of 1.3 and changes nothing about the split.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-what-the-meter-and-the-model-say', 13, 'On the teaching field at a maxTestAgeDays of 0 the last-day oil factor is 0.909283141463 and the diagnostics are 42 no_test_in_force against 57 at the default. Why is that pair of readouts a trap?', '["Because the guard is Number.isFinite(x) and x > 0, so 0 turns the age check off, and the run with the nearest factor and the thinnest diagnostic list is the run with no age limit applied.", "Because the diagnostics count events rather than wells, so 42 and 57 are not comparable across two runs.", "Because the factor is a daily figure and the diagnostics are a whole-run total.", "Because a factor below one and a factor above one cannot be ranked for closeness."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Turning the dial down through 120 and 60 raises the diagnostics to 65 and then 86 and pushes the factor to 1.348582874057, and then 0 reverses both readouts at once.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-what-the-meter-and-the-model-say', 14, 'A run returns a no_test_in_force count of 57. What does that number tell you about which wells were affected?', '["That 57 wells had no carrier at some point in the window, since the code fires once for each well it affects.", "That 57 dates in the allocated window had no carrier, since the code fires once for each date that lost one.", "That the count is the number of barrels the missing tests would have carried into the split had they been in force.", "Nothing, because diagnosticCounts is a tally with no well identifiers and no dates on it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The code fires on a well and a date together, so 57 could be one well for a long time or many wells briefly and the return does not say which.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-what-the-meter-and-the-model-say', 15, 'A derived two-well demonstration raises three factor_out_of_band diagnostics when SHARE-B files no row and none at all when SHARE-B is filed as shut in. What is the code reporting?', '["That a well was missing from the date, which is the cause of the factor moving.", "That a factor left the band, which is the symptom, and not that a well with no row was credited with a full day and took half the metered oil.", "That the metered total disagreed with the sum of the ledger rows for the date.", "That the split could not be made and the date was left unallocated."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The factor moved to exactly 0.500000000000 and SHARE-A, which had made every barrel the meter saw, was allocated 500.000000 stb of the 1000.000000 stb it made.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 1, 'What is the one sentence this tier ends on?', '["A surveillance number is a measurement of a well and an allocated number is a correction applied to that measurement before it is booked.", "A surveillance number is a rate and an allocated number is a volume, so the two are simply reported in different units.", "A surveillance number is metered and an allocated number is modelled, and the pair brackets what the well truly made.", "A surveillance number is a difference and an allocated number is a share, and neither measures the well whose name is beside it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every number in the tier is the distance between two others, and the second quantity is chosen by whoever wrote the call: a baseline window, a test rate or a facility meter.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 2, 'The capstone order is test in force, then uptime, then theoretical, then factor, then allocated share. What happens if a step is skipped?', '["The run refuses, because each step validates the one before it.", "The arithmetic still works and produces a number that means something else.", "The factor is returned as null, which is how a missing step is reported.", "The share is written from the ledger row instead, which is the documented fallback."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Nothing in the return names the step that was skipped, which is why the order is worth working in rather than assuming.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 3, 'Step two says to find the anchor before building a window. What is the anchor on the teaching field?', '["Today''s date, so that a window described as recent really is recent.", "2024-10-15, the first day of the baseline window, from which both windows are measured forward.", "2024-11-20, the field''s latest ledger date, giving a recent window of 2024-11-14 to 2024-11-20.", "The last date on which every well filed a row, so that no window is averaged over a partial field."]'::jsonb, 2, 'asOf is returned so a reader can tell what the word recent meant, and the baseline that goes with it runs 2024-10-15 to 2024-11-13.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 4, 'Step six says to name which number is being quoted. On 2024-11-20 OGUTA-2 carries 1036.000000000 stb, 1233.319797595 stb and 1014.000000000 stb. Which is which?', '["The theoretical, the allocated share and the well''s own ledger row, in that order, and all three are calendar volumes over one date.", "The ledger row, the theoretical and the allocated share, since a theoretical always sits between a well''s own measurement and the share a meter produced for it.", "The allocated share, the metered total for the well and the ledger row, all of them modelled.", "The producing-day rate, the calendar volume and the theoretical, which is why the three disagree."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The theoretical is the test rate of 1036.000000 stb/d times an uptime of 1.000000000, and it is the one of the three that no meter ever saw.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 5, 'A capstone check says a well with no test in force has no share. What is the distinction being drawn?', '["No allocated volume rather than no theoretical, since the theoretical is still formed and carried.", "No entry at all rather than an entry of zero, with a no_test_in_force diagnostic in its place.", "No share on that date rather than none for the run, since a well can return to the split later.", "No oil share rather than none on the other two phases, which are allocated separately."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The teaching run records 57 of those diagnostics across 21 days, and only four wells of eight ever take a share.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 6, 'The tier''s list of failures to expect includes quoting the theoretical because it sits closer to the ledger row. Why is that a failure?', '["It is a rate in stb/d rather than a volume in stb, so it cannot be compared with a ledger row over a date without a scaling nobody applied.", "It is formed after the factor, so it already carries into every well the same field-wide correction that the allocated share carries.", "It is taken from a test the QC may well have flagged, which the allocated share is not, so it carries an unchecked reading into the answer.", "It is a test rate scaled by an uptime, so it is just as modelled and it is the one of the three that no meter ever saw."]'::jsonb, 3, 'On 2024-11-20 the theoretical of 1036.000000000 stb sits nearer the ledger row of 1014.000000000 stb than the allocated 1233.319797595 stb does, and being nearer is not being measured.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 7, 'validateWellTests returns an empty array on a run. What has that established?', '["Either every test was clean or no test was checked, because the return carries no count of tests examined.", "That every test passed, which is what an empty issue list means.", "That the tests were all dropped by their is_valid flag before the QC ran.", "That the QC could not run at all, since a test with no issue on it is still returned as a row carrying an empty code list."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The published case hands in 7 tests and returns 4 rows, and QC on an empty list also returns 0 rows.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 8, 'Which of the tier''s five claims would change if the same ledger were reread a month later with no new rows added?', '["All of them, because a month of elapsed time moves the anchor forward and therefore moves both of the windows with it.", "The staleness rows only, since a well''s silence is measured against the clock and grows by a day for every day that passes.", "None of the surveillance windows, because every one of them anchors on the field''s latest ledger date.", "The allocation only, since a test in force ages by a month against maxTestAgeDays."]'::jsonb, 2, 'One function does move: summarizeDeferments substitutes today''s date when its asOf is omitted, and an open event accrues days to it.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 9, 'What did the Professional tier deliberately not settle?', '["Which of the seven exception types can reach high, which the Expert tier resolves.", "Which reading a period ratio is, since a watercut and a gas-oil ratio can each be formed two defensible ways from identical rows.", "Whether a factor should be clamped into the warning band before the shares are written.", "Whether an allocation should be run on the test basis or on the ledger basis."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Two functions in one file disagree about it, and nothing in either return says which reading it took.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 10, 'The Expert preview prices the ratio seam on the published well: 70.033482142857 per cent one way and 42.737789203085 per cent the other. What is the 19.122961825433 per cent beside them?', '["The overstatement of one reading against the other, published by the golden as a measured disagreement rather than resolved.", "The gas-oil ratio trigger the pair is measured against, doubled to high.", "The share of the recent window in which the two readings differ at all.", "The tolerance inside which the two readings are treated as agreeing."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The first reading prints high and the second prints medium, so the seam moves a printed severity on rows that nobody touched.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 11, 'The Expert preview says OGUTA-6 holds a producing-day oil rate of 512.000000000000 stb/d across its recent window while its mean calendar oil falls from 502.666666666667 stb to 307.504761904762 stb. What did the engine print?', '["A downtime at medium, because the mean recent hours of 14.414285714286 h are the real cause of the fall in the volume column.", "Nothing at all, because the two columns disagree with each other and the comparison on that well is skipped rather than reported.", "A shut_in at high, because a fall of that size on a producer is read as a stop rather than as a fall in the rate it was making.", "A rate drop of 38.825312618416 per cent, on a well whose producing-day rate rose by 1.856763925729 per cent."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The downtime exception that would have named the cause does not fire either, since 14.414285714286 h sits above the threshold of 12 h.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 12, 'Putting the refused tests back into the teaching allocation moves the theoretical oil from 54713.920833333 stb to 61028.920833333 stb. What does the last-day oil factor do?', '["It moves from 1.190463125091 to 1.348582874057, since more theoretical volume raises the ratio.", "It is unchanged, because the factor is a property of the metered stream.", "It moves from 1.190463125091 to 1.001040970439 on an unchanged meter.", "It becomes null on the dates the refused tests carry, which is what a no_basis marks."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A factor that close to one looks like agreement between the tests and the meter, and it was produced by admitting the tests the default had refused.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 13, 'annualEffectiveDecline is handed a hyperbolic fit whose exponent is NaN. The Expert preview says it returns 42.160601062199 per cent. What is that number?', '["The exponential answer at the same nominal decline of 0.0015 per day, because the guard treats an unusable exponent as falsy.", "The hyperbolic answer with the exponent replaced by one, the harmonic case.", "A refusal encoded as a percentage, which the caller is expected to recognise.", "The decline over the fitted window rather than over a year."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A nominal decline is per DAY and an effective decline is a per cent over the first year, and the function evaluates the rate law at 365.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 14, 'liftScreening documents its target rate as LIQUID and liftAdvisor uses the identical input as OIL. At 300.000000000 bbl/d of oil and a water cut of 70.000000 per cent, what does the Expert preview report?', '["A liquid rate of 300.000000000 bbl/d, the oil number passed straight through, and a rod pump score that does not move.", "Two scores that differ only in the band they land in, since a screening score is a ranking device and not a probability.", "A refusal, because the two modules disagree about the phase of the number they were handed and neither of them can proceed.", "A liquid rate of 1000.000000000000 bbl/d, and a rod pump scoring 80 read one way and 40 read the other."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A watercut is a 0 to 1 fraction in surveillance and allocation and a PER CENT in the two lift modules, and the shipped studio passes one number to both.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 15, 'What stays in force from this tier once the Expert tier begins?', '["Nothing, because the Expert tier re-reads the same rows a second way and the first reading is superseded.", "Windows still anchor on the data, a factor is still an output, and closure still proves only that one factor multiplied every share.", "Only the allocation results, since the surveillance half is re-derived on the producing-day column.", "Only the thresholds, since the Expert tier changes which function forms each ratio."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Expert tier introduces no new data at all: it re-runs the same calls under a different reading of the identical rows.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 1, 'OGUTA-17 stops filing ledger rows on the teaching field and keeps producing. Surveillance and allocation both meet that silence. What does each make of it?', '["Surveillance raises stale_data at medium and allocation credits the well with a full 24 h day and hands it a share.", "Both read it as a well off stream, so the exception is raised and the well takes no share on those dates.", "Surveillance skips the well as unsurveillable and allocation raises a no_basis on every date it is missing.", "Surveillance raises stale_data and allocation refuses the date entirely, which is why the grand total stops closing."]'::jsonb, 0, 'One well, two modules and two opposite readings of the same missing rows: value = 23.000000000000 days against a baseline of 7.000000000000 in the one, and six allocated dates on an uptime of 1.000000000 in the other.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 2, 'An allocation engineer walks maxTestAgeDays down from 180 to 120 to 60 and then to 0, watching the last-day oil factor and the diagnostic list. What does the walk end in?', '["A factor of 1.348582874057 and 86 diagnostics, the tightest limit giving the worst pair of readouts.", "A run that refuses, because a limit of 0 admits no test on file.", "A factor of 0.909283141463 and 42 no_test_in_force diagnostics, both the healthiest readouts on the sweep, produced by a setting that turns the age check off.", "A factor of 1.190463125091 unchanged from the default, since the last day''s carriers are all recent."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The guard is Number.isFinite(maxTestAgeDays) and maxTestAgeDays > 0, so 0 fails it, and the theoretical oil rises to 65033.920833333 stb on tests the default had already thrown out.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 3, 'OGUTA-5 raises a high watercut_rise on the teaching field, value = 0.867469879518 against a baseline of 0.280000000000. How much of the metered oil is it allocated over the 21 days?', '["A share proportional to its baseline oil mean of 3.600000000000 stb, which is small but not zero.", "None, because it has no test in force on any of those dates and a well with no carrier takes no share.", "A share on the dates its watercut exception covers, since an exception is what puts a well into the split.", "A zero share written into the return, which is how the split records a well it could not carry."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Four wells of eight take any share at all, and the surveillance half and the allocation half select wells on entirely different grounds.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 4, 'OGUTA-6 is printed at medium as a rate drop while its mean recent hours are 14.414285714286 h. Which two settings decided that pair of outcomes?', '["minOilRate at 5 and staleDays at 7, the two gates that decide whether a well is compared at all.", "rateDropPct at 20 and watercutRisePts at 10, since a fall in oil raises the watercut on the same rows.", "downtimeHours at 12 and gorRisePct at 30, the two thresholds that read the hours column.", "rateDropPct at 20, which the calendar fall of 38.825312618416 per cent clears without doubling, and downtimeHours at 12, which 14.414285714286 h sits above."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The producing-day oil rate of 512.000000000000 stb/d against a baseline of 502.666666666667 stb/d is the column that would have settled it, and no exception reads it.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 5, 'A field''s allocation closes exactly, 58426.784897363 stb measured against 58426.784897363 stb allocated. What does the imbalance series say about the same field?', '["Nothing, because the imbalance is computed from the allocated volumes rather than the booked ones, and a run that closes has no imbalance left in it.", "That the imbalance is zero on every date, which is what closure means expressed as a percentage.", "That the meter saw 437.869938006 stb more than the wells booked on the last date, at 19.806426169154 per cent of the booked volume.", "That the imbalance equals the difference between the theoretical and the allocated oil."]'::jsonb, 2, 'imbalanceSeries compares the facility meter against what the wells booked in their own ledger rows and never looks at an allocation, so a split cannot flatter it.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 6, 'Two QC routines could have caught teaching test g-o9-1. What actually happened to it?', '["validateWellTests called it a watercut_mismatch at medium, the nodal cross-check would have needed a well model, and the test carried OGUTA-9 for all 21 allocated days regardless.", "It was excluded from the split by its QC severity, which is what a medium verdict is for.", "It was replaced by the ledger row on its test date, which is the documented fallback.", "It was kept but down-weighted, so OGUTA-9 took a reduced share on the dates it carried."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A theoretical oil of 19488.000000000 stb and an allocated oil of 20995.477445353 stb came out of a test the same file had flagged, because groupTests reads is_valid and nothing else.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 7, 'Every surveillance window on the teaching field anchors at 2024-11-20. What is the deferment book anchored on?', '["The same 2024-11-20, since every function in the file shares one anchor.", "The first date of the deferment events, so the roll-up covers its own span.", "The last date on which an event was closed, so an open event never accrues.", "Whatever asOf it is given, and today''s date when the argument is omitted, which is what makes it the one function that can answer differently tomorrow."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Anchored at 2024-11-20 the open event is 173 days and at 2025-06-30 it is 395 days, and its oil is 3200.000000 stb at both.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 8, 'OGUTA-14 files one row a month and its windows widen to 45, 120 and 45 days. What is its position in the allocation?', '["It is allocated on a widened basis, since the split widens its window on the cadence the way the exception check does.", "It has no test in force on any allocated date, so it takes no share and contributes no_test_in_force diagnostics.", "It is allocated on its own ledger rows, since a well filing one row a month has no test the split could use.", "It is allocated a monthly share which allocatedLedgerRows then writes back as 30 daily rows."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The widening lives in detectExceptions alone, so the same coarse well that is reported at high severity for producing more oil per elapsed day is in no split at all.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 9, 'A surveillance exception and an allocation diagnostic are put side by side. What do both refuse to name?', '["The severity, which the exception carries as a rank and the diagnostic does not carry in any form at all.", "The setting that produced them, and in the diagnostic''s case the well and the date as well.", "The window, which neither of them uses, since both are formed over the whole ledger rather than over a range.", "The phase, which the diagnostic carries on every count and the exception leaves out of its message entirely."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A list of 8 exceptions and a list of 9 on identical data differ only by a dial, and a no_test_in_force count of 57 could be one well for a long time or many wells briefly.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 10, 'Setting includeInvalidTests to true on the teaching field puts g-o17-2 back into the candidate pool. Trace it through to the factor.', '["The theoretical falls, because an invalid test is a low reading and it pulls the sum down.", "The allocated oil rises to match the new theoretical, since the shares are the theoreticals times a factor.", "Nothing moves on the last day, because g-o17-2 is dated 2024-11-06 and cannot carry a later date.", "The theoretical oil rises to 61028.920833333 stb, the last-day oil factor falls to 1.001040970439 and the no_test_in_force count falls from 57 to 42."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The QC had called that test a short_duration at medium on 2.5 h, and the setting that admits it is not the QC''s to set.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 11, 'A two-well demonstration credits SHARE-B, which filed no row at all, with half the metered oil. Which diagnostic fires, and what does it name?', '["factor_out_of_band, three times, and it names a factor of 0.500000000000 leaving the band rather than the missing row that caused it.", "no_basis, once for each phase on the date, and it names the date whose theoretical came out at zero and therefore had nothing at all to divide.", "no_test_in_force, and it names SHARE-B as the well without a carrier on the date.", "None, because a well credited with defaultHours has a complete basis and nothing to report."]'::jsonb, 0, 'SHARE-A actually made every barrel the meter saw, and told the truth about SHARE-B with an hours_on of 0 the run gives a factor of 1.000000000000 and no diagnostic.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 12, 'The teaching field runs 2024-09-12 to 2024-11-20 and its deferment roll-up is quoted at asOf 2024-11-20. Why does the wave anchor it by hand?', '["Because the roll-up needs an end date for its categories to be sorted by oil.", "Because the open event would otherwise be dropped from the count of events.", "Because summarizeDeferments defaults its asOf to today, in a file whose other windows anchor on the field''s latest ledger date so an old dataset surveils honestly.", "Because the deferred oil moves with the anchor and has to be fixed before it is quoted."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Only the days move with the anchor: 29 days and 12800.000000 stb of oil at 2024-11-20, and the volumes are filed figures that no anchor touches.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 13, 'Surveillance ranks OGUTA-17''s silence among the mediums. What is the same silence worth on the meter?', '["Nothing, because a well that files no rows also books no volume and drops out of both sides of the comparison.", "The difference between its theoretical and its allocated oil, which the split reports as a residual.", "A gap that grows by 688.000000 stb a day, the rate of the test that was carrying it.", "Over 400 stb of unaccounted oil a day, 437.869938006 stb on 2024-11-20 alone."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The stale_data row that says the data has stopped arriving sorts below every high rate drop on the field and is ordered among the mediums alphabetically.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 14, 'Tightening maxTestAgeDays from 180 to 60 on the teaching field takes the wells with a share from 4 to 2 and the theoretical oil from 54713.920833333 stb to 39172.000000000 stb. What happens to the allocated oil?', '["It falls in proportion to the theoretical, since each share written is a theoretical multiplied by that date''s factor.", "It stays at 58426.784897363 stb, because the metered stream is what is being divided and only the divisor moved.", "It falls to the share the two remaining wells can carry, and the rest of the metered oil is reported as no_basis.", "It rises, because two wells absorbing the same meter are credited with more each."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The same metered oil divided among fewer wells is what pushes the last-day oil factor to 1.348582874057, and 35 factor_out_of_band diagnostics come with it.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 15, 'How many of the seven exception types can reach high severity?', '["Four, which is the number of high rows the published field happens to return at the default settings.", "Seven, because every one of the types has a doubled threshold sitting behind its own trigger.", "Five, since shut_in is high unconditionally and four types double their trigger.", "Three, since only the two rate types and the shut_in are scored on a size rather than on a name."]'::jsonb, 2, 'rate_drop, injection_drop, watercut_rise and gor_rise double, shut_in is always high, and only downtime and stale_data cannot get there, which is a count of TYPES and not of rows.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 16, 'Two wells on one field come back with rate_drop rows in the same list. What can be said about the windows behind them?', '["They may be of different lengths, because each well widens its own windows on its own cadence and no row says which it used.", "They are identical, since recentDays and baselineDays are field settings that every well on the run is compared under.", "They are identical in length and merely offset by each well''s own last reporting date, which is the reason an anchor is returned.", "They differ only when a well is monthly, and the return marks such a row."]'::jsonb, 0, 'At a cadence of 1.0 or 2.0 days the defaults of 7, 30 and 7 stand, and at 30.5 days the same well gets 46, 122 and 46.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 17, 'seriesCadenceDays is handed a well with a single ledger point. What does it return?', '["null, because a median gap needs at least two points to have a gap between them.", "1.0, the default cadence, so the widening leaves the windows at 7, 30 and 7 days as they stand.", "0.0, since the only gap such a series contains is the one from the single point to itself.", "The field cadence, which is the median of the per-well cadences taken across the whole field."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A cadence of null leaves nothing for the widening maxima to act on, and the well is compared over the default windows.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 18, 'Setting recentDays to 1 on the published field gives 8 exceptions with 2 high and 6 medium against 9 with 4 high at the default of 7. What does a one-day recent window mean for a mean?', '["It is an empty window, since a half-open range of one day contains no rows.", "It is the same as a seven-day window, because the mean of a window is a mean either way.", "It is a rolling mean of the seven most recent rows, which is what the module substitutes below 7.", "It is a single row, so the comparison is one day against a thirty-day baseline and a single ordinary day can clear a well of a change that ran all week."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The bad days that produced the high rows at the default are still in the ledger, and at a recent window of 1 day most of them are not being looked at.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 19, 'Why does detectExceptions return asOf in its result at all?', '["Because the caller has to pass it back in to reproduce the same list.", "Because it is the date the exception rows were computed on, which the audit trail needs.", "Because a reader cannot otherwise tell what the word recent meant on that run.", "Because the allocation reads it to align its own window with the surveillance one."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Two runs over the same wells, one before a late row arrives and one after, place both windows a day apart and nothing else in the object says so.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 20, 'The shut_in type is described as producing in the baseline window and nothing in the test window. What size of baseline does it need?', '["A baseline above 40 per cent of the field mean, the doubled rate trigger.", "Any baseline above minOilRate, since its severity is high whatever the number was.", "A baseline above 400 stb, the value the published case records.", "A baseline at least twice the recent mean, which is what makes the stop a doubling."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The published P-2 row is a stop from a baseline of 400.000000000000 stb and it outranks a fall to 555.714285714286 stb from 900.000000000000 stb on another well.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 21, 'minOilRate defaults to 5. Which mean is it applied to?', '["The recent mean, so a well that has already collapsed falls under the gate and drops out of the rate check on that account.", "The mean of the two windows taken together, so a well is gated on its whole record rather than on either window alone.", "The BASELINE mean, so a well that has already fallen below it is still compared on the rate the baseline window recorded.", "The field mean, so the gate is the same number for every well in the run."]'::jsonb, 2, 'On the published field the sweep is not monotonic: at 400 the list is 8 rows and at 20000 it is 3, and a watercut_rise survives every setting because the gate does not reach it.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 22, 'On the published field, downtimeHours of 0, 1, 4 and 8 all give 8 exceptions and 12 gives 9. What is the extra row?', '["A stale_data on P-4, which the lower thresholds had suppressed, because the two checks are reading the same hours column.", "The downtime on P-5, which averages 8.000000000000 h and only crosses a threshold once the threshold is above its mean.", "A second rate_drop on P-5, since a downtime and a rate drop sit behind the same minOilRate gate on one and the same well.", "A shut_in on P-2, which is reported as downtime below a threshold of 12."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The message reads that the well is averaging 8.0 hours on stream against a 12-hour threshold, and the severity is medium at every setting that produces it.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 23, 'A staleDays of 10 on the published field gives 9 exceptions of which one is info. What has happened to the stale row?', '["Its gap of 20 days clears the trigger without passing the doubled one, so it prints at info.", "It has been demoted because the staleDays setting is above the default of 7 and a raised trigger softens the row.", "It is a second stale row on a different well, and the original row on P-4 stays where it was at medium severity.", "It has dropped to info because the well filed a row inside the window that the raised setting widened for it."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Doubling staleDays moves that type from info to medium and no further, so info and medium are the whole ladder it has.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 24, 'Raising gorRisePct to 60 on the teaching field leaves 8 exceptions. What moved?', '["The OGUTA-2 watercut row, which shares the ratio reading with the gas-oil row.", "The OGUTA-5 watercut row, which is the only other ratio exception on the field.", "Nothing, because a rise of 83.907484614181 per cent clears any setting the sweep uses.", "The OGUTA-2 gas-oil ratio row, from high to medium, on rows that did not change."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A rise of 83.907484614181 per cent is above 60 and below the doubled 120, so the same rows print one rank lower.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 25, 'At a rateDropPct of 10 the teaching field returns OGUTA-3W at injection_drop and high. At the default of 20 it is medium. What is its change?', '["-40.500254390 per cent, which is the same figure that OGUTA-2 records on its own oil column over the same recent window.", "-30.106809079 per cent, a recent mean injection of 1994.285714285714 stb against a baseline of 2853.333333333333 stb.", "-38.825312618 per cent, the figure carried by the other medium row that the same teaching field returns on the same run.", "-52.000000000 per cent, which is what the message on the coarse well reports."]'::jsonb, 1, 'rate_drop and injection_drop share one setting, so a producer and an injector cross the same trigger and its doubling at the same numbers.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 26, 'Published well w-p2 has a test in force on 2024-12-20 at a maxTestAgeDays of 120 and none on 2025-01-25 at the same setting. What changed?', '["Nothing about the tests, since the well filed none in between: the same test simply aged past 120 days as the probe date moved forward.", "A newer test took over on the later date and was itself already outside the 120 day limit on the day that it came into force.", "The well left the split, which is what removes a test from force.", "The limit is measured from the first probe date rather than from each date in turn, so the later date falls outside the window."]'::jsonb, 0, 'At 180 days the same well returns t-p2-a at all six probe dates, so the limit alone decides whether the well is in the split.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 27, 'A well''s only test is dated after the day being probed. What does testInForce return?', '["The future test, since it is the most recent measurement anyone has taken and the nearest in time to the probe date.", "The field''s mean rate for the date, scaled by the well''s uptime, which is what the no_test_in_force diagnostic records.", "The test, carried backwards to the probe date with a diagnostic marking the answer as an extrapolation rather than a reading.", "null, because it never looks forward and will not take the nearest test when the nearest one is in the future."]'::jsonb, 3, 'It also never averages two tests and never extrapolates a decline between them, so a rate steps on a date and holds flat.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 28, 'The published QC returns q-7 with codes zero_rate and no_ledger and an overall severity of high. Where does the high come from?', '["no_ledger, since a test that cannot be cross-checked is the more serious of the two.", "The pair together, since two codes on one test raise it a rank.", "zero_rate, since the no_ledger code on the same test is only info.", "The duration, since a test that recorded no flow is treated as a short test."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A test row takes the highest severity among its codes, and the message on the zero rate reads that the test recorded no flow at all.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 29, 'The teaching field hands 6 tests to the QC and gets 5 rows back, of which 3 carry no_ledger at info alone. What do those 3 rows establish?', '["That the tests are clean, since info is the rank the QC gives a test that carries no real issue against any of its codes.", "That the tests were filed against dates outside the range the daily ledger covers and are unusable in a split.", "That the ledger comparison ran and found no mismatch worth reporting.", "That the test date had no ledger row, so the two ledger comparisons did not run and their silence says nothing."]'::jsonb, 3, 'No ledger row means no ledger_mismatch and no watercut_mismatch, and the severity column will not tell you that a check was skipped rather than passed.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 30, 'The q-4 message reads that test oil of 1,500 stb/d stands against 520 stb/d in the ledger, 188% apart. Which setting decided that this was worth printing?', '["outlierPct at 50, which is the setting behind every rate comparison in the QC.", "watercutTolerancePts at 10, since the row also carries a watercut mismatch.", "ledgerTolerancePct, at a default of 30 per cent, which the comparison passed by a wide margin.", "minDurationHours at 4, since a comparison is only made on a test long enough to have stabilized."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The same test also reads 200% off this well''s 500 stb/d test median, which is the separate outlierPct comparison against the well''s own history.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 31, 'Running the published allocation on the ledger basis gives a theoretical oil of 39980.000000000 stb and a grand factor of 0.992896448224. Is that the best of the five runs?', '["Yes, because a factor that close to one means the wells were measured correctly and the split then needed almost no correction at all.", "It is the closest to one, and closeness to one is a statement about the models agreeing with the meter and not about either being right.", "No, because a ledger basis allocates a well its own rows and cannot be checked.", "Yes, because it also raises the fewest factor_out_of_band diagnostics of the five."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The five runs are the same field and the same meter at a measured oil of 39696.000000000 stb, and their factors run from 0.961162227603 to 1.686321155480.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 32, 'The teaching allocation on the ledger basis returns a theoretical oil of 48401.909585077 stb and a single factor_out_of_band diagnostic. Why so few?', '["Because the theoretical is now built from the wells'' own rows, which track the meter closely, so the daily factors stay inside the band.", "Because the ledger basis skips the wells with no test in force, which is what the diagnostic counts.", "Because a ledger basis clamps the factor into the band before the shares are written.", "Because the run allocates fewer days, so there are fewer factors to fall outside."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The default test basis records 57 no_test_in_force diagnostics on the same 21 days, and the ledger basis records none of them at all.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 33, 'The published allocationWithInvalidTests run has 3 wells taking a share against 2 at the default. What did admitting the refused tests do to the factor?', '["It raised it from 1.043350271596 to 1.193984359334, on a theoretical of 33246.666666667 stb.", "It lowered it towards one, because a third well in the split spreads the same metered oil across more theoretical volume.", "It left it unchanged, since the metered oil of 39696.000000000 stb is the numerator and it did not move between the runs.", "It raised it because the measured oil rose with the extra well taking a share of the same metered stream."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The no_test_in_force count falls from 24 to 3 in the same run, so more wells carried and the sum of what they claimed still fell.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 34, 'In the published monthly factors, P-1 comes back at 1.009994621755 for 2025-02-01 and P-2 at 1.216689570872 for the same month. Why do two wells on one field differ?', '["Because the two wells were allocated under different settings in that month.", "Because a monthly factor is one well''s own allocated volume over its own theoretical volume, so it carries the mix of days that well was in the split.", "Because the daily factors are per well, and the monthly figure is their mean.", "Because P-2 has a phase with no theoretical, which carries a factor of 1."]'::jsonb, 1, 'In January the same pair reads 0.941586239847 and 0.939104708001, which is what a month of shared days looks like.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 35, 'OGUTA-17 has a November monthly oil factor of 0.963317484735 while the other three teaching wells sit near 1.08. What is behind the gap?', '["Its test was invalid, so its monthly factor was computed from the well''s own ledger rows for the month instead of from a test rate.", "Its oil phase had no theoretical volume for the month at all, so its factor falls back to the documented nothing to scale value.", "It was in the split for only part of the month, so its factor carries a different mix of daily factors from the others.", "It was allocated on a widened window, which averages more days into its factor."]'::jsonb, 2, 'That well takes a share on 6 days of the 21 and its allocated oil for the run is 3965.831869672 stb on a theoretical of 4128.000000000 stb.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 36, 'On the teaching field the oil factor is 0.966380008629 on 2024-11-05 with 4 entries and 1.212710044537 on 2024-11-06 with 3. What happened between the two dates?', '["A well was shut in, so its theoretical volume went to zero for the date and the factor rose to compensate for it.", "The meter was recalibrated overnight, which is the only thing that could move a factor between two consecutive dates.", "A new test came into force at a lower rate on one well, which lowered the sum of the theoreticals for the date.", "A test aged out, so a well left the split and the same metered oil was divided among fewer theoreticals."]'::jsonb, 3, 'OGUTA-17''s carrier g-o17-1 is 180 days old on 2024-11-05 and outside the limit on 2024-11-06, and the replacement test on that date was filed with is_valid false.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 37, 'On 2024-10-31 the teaching field''s oil imbalance is 522.187140049 stb at 20.808289025167 per cent, and on 2024-11-20 it is 437.869938006 stb at 19.806426169154 per cent. What has the field been doing in between?', '["Closing the gap steadily, since both the unaccounted barrels and the percentage fell across the span.", "Losing meter accuracy, since a percentage that sits near twenty on every date is the signature of a systematic bias.", "Booking less oil in its ledger while the meter kept seeing volume the wells did not account for.", "Allocating the difference back to the wells, which is why the unaccounted barrels fell over the span."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Between those dates the percentage runs as high as 31.931380703618 while the unaccounted barrels barely move, because the denominator is what the wells booked.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 38, 'The published nodal check reports n-ok at -9.997699132922 per cent against a tolerancePct of 35. What has that established about the test?', '["That the test is correct, since the well model independently reproduced the rate it recorded to within the tolerance the caller set.", "That the well model is correct, since the test reproduced it.", "That the test passed every check in the domain, since the nodal cross-check is the strongest QC there is and it cleared the test.", "That the measured rate of 1180.000000000 stb/d and the model''s 1311.077593164 stb/d agree inside the tolerance the caller set."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The five statuses are dead, off, no-thp, no-model and ok, and ok means the deviation was inside a tolerance and nothing more.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 39, 'The teaching deferment book puts Facility first with 2 events, 7 days and 5820.000000 stb of oil, above Artificial lift with 1 event and 10 days. What would reorder them?', '["Sorting by days rather than by oil, since Artificial lift has the longer outage at 3140.000000 stb.", "Sorting by event count, which would put Facility first on 2 events and leave Artificial lift below it.", "Anchoring the roll-up at a later date, which grows the open event''s day count and moves its category up.", "Nothing, because the sort is fixed at oil and then days and Facility leads on the first of those two keys."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The sort is oil descending and then days, which is the right order for a loss review and the wrong one for a maintenance backlog.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 40, 'An operator reads a list of 8 exceptions on the teaching field and works down it as a work order. What has that reading assumed?', '["That every well on the field was compared, which is true of 8 wells of 8.", "That the order reflects the size of the problem, when it reflects a severity rank and then an alphabet, under one set of settings, on the calendar volume.", "That the settings were the defaults, which the return states beside each row.", "That the exceptions are one per well, which the list is built to guarantee."]'::jsonb, 1, 'OGUTA-2 appears three times in that list, OGUTA-21 was never compared at all, and the medium block holds a well whose producing-day rate did not fall.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 41, 'A theoretical volume, an allocated volume and a ledger volume for one well on one date are all quoted in stb. What separates them?', '["The theoretical and the allocated are volumes and the ledger row is a producing-day rate.", "The theoretical is a test rate scaled by an uptime, the allocated is that times a field factor, and the ledger row is what the well itself filed.", "The theoretical is metered at the well itself, the allocated is metered at the facility separator, and the ledger row is the modelled one of the three.", "The allocated carries the uptime and the other two do not."]'::jsonb, 1, 'All three are calendar volumes over one date, and on 2024-11-20 OGUTA-2 reads 1036.000000000, 1233.319797595 and 1014.000000000 stb.'),
  ('surveillance', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 42, 'What is the one thing an exactly closing allocated day proves?', '["That the metered total was correctly divided among the wells that produced it.", "That the tests in force on the date agreed with the facility meter.", "That every share on the date was the same factor times a theoretical.", "That the uptimes used on the date matched the hours the wells actually ran."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The published days print a closure residual of 0.000000000000 whatever the tests said, and a date with no basis is not in the grand total at all.');
